          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em reflexive object
            is a model of the pure (untyped)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            validating the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            law, and sometimes also the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            law.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong reflexive object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            equipped with a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %munder
                              %mo ⇄
                              %mi lam
                            %mi app
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi U
                            %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{U &amp; \overset{app}{\underset{lam}{\rightleftarrows}} &amp; U^U}
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{(-)}
            denotes the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi app
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi lam
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app \circ lam = 1
          %p
            In other words, a reflexive object is an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            together with data
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                  %mo ⊲
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U \lhd U
            exhibiting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            A reflexive object is said to be
            %strong extensional
            (or “strict”) when also
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi lam
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi app
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lam \circ app = 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi U
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U \cong U
          %p
            Viewed as a model of lambda calculus, the equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi lam
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app \circ lam = 1
            of a reflexive object represents
            = succeed "-equality" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.t)(u) = t[u/x]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lam
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi app
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lam \circ app = 1
            of an extensional reflexive object represents
            = succeed "-equality" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.t(x) = t
          %h3#in_a_closed_symmetric_monoidal_category In a closed (symmetric) monoidal category
          %p
            The definition has a straightforward generalization to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+closed+category"} symmetric monoidal closed category
            (i.e., not necessarily
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [U,U]
          %p
            A reflexive object in a symmetric monoidal closed category provides a model of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                linear lambda calculus
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/linear+lambda+calculus"} ?
          %h3#in_a_closed_monoidal_2category In a closed monoidal 2-category
          %p
            The definition of reflexive object also has a natural generalization to any closed monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            (or cartesian closed 2-category), where the retraction should be replaced by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi app
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi lam
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app \dashv lam : [U,U] \to U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"} counit
            of the adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi lam
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app \circ lam \Rightarrow 1_{[U,U]}
            models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.t)(u) \to t[u/x]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi U
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi lam
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi app
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_U \Rightarrow lam \circ app
            models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-expansion"} eta-expansion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo →
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \to \lambda x.t(x)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#terminal_object Terminal object
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            of a ccc provides a degenerate example of a (extensional) reflexive object, and for cardinality reasons, this is the only reflexive object in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %h3#scotts__construction
            Scott’s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_\infty
            construction
          %p
            The first non-degenerate model of untyped lambda calculus was described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"} Dana Scott
            (see
            %a{:href => "#Scott70"} Scott (1970)
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Scott72"} Scott (1972)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+lattices"} algebraic lattices
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+topology"} Scott-continuous
            functions. The so-called “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_\infty
            ” model is built by a limit construction, starting from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_0 = D
            an arbitrary algebraic lattice, and taking
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{n+1} = D_n \to D_n
          %h3#enumeration_operator_model Enumeration operator model
          %p
            Scott later gave a more concrete model of lambda calculus in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Scott76"} Scott (1976)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\omega
            of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of the non-negative integers, and with the maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lam
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lam
            (there called “fun” and “graph”) defined as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo ∃
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app(u)(x) = \{m \mid \exists e_n \subseteq x. (n,m) \in u\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lam
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lam(f) = \{(n,m) \mid m \in f(e_n)\}
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_n
            stands for the set whose elements are the exponents in the binary expansion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_n
            is the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,m)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mi m
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,m) = \frac{1}{2}(n+m)(n+m+1)+m
          %p
            Note that the enumeration operator model is not “extensional” in the sense that it only validates
            = succeed "-conversion" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \subseteq \ell(a(u))
          %h3#scotts_representation_theorem Scott’s representation theorem
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Scott80"} Scott (1980)
            proved a sort of representation theorem for pure lambda calculus, showing that
            %em any
            “type-free theory” (a.k.a.,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+theory"} lambda theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            such that one can prove
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \lambda x.A(A(x))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
            are (equivalence classes of) closed terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            such that one can prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = \lambda x.B(f(A(x)))
            (cf.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+envelope"} Karoubi envelope
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1_A = A
                  \\qquad
                  f \circ g = \lambda x.f(g(x))
          %p This category is cartesian closed, where products and exponentials are defined (on objects) by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi u
                  %mi λ
                  %mi z
                  %mo .
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mi λ
                  %mi y
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mi λ
                  %mi y
                  %mo .
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \times B = \lambda u\lambda z.z(A(u(\lambda x\lambda y.x)))(B(u(\lambda x\lambda y.y)))
                  \\qquad
                  A \to B = \lambda f.B \circ f \circ A
          %p
            Now, the identity term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \lambda x.x
            lives inside this category since obviously
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = U\circ U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a retract of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : A \to U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : U \to A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \circ A = A = 1_A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            equipped with its retraction from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to U
            is a reflexive object.
          %p
            A more abstract analysis of Scott’s representation theorem appears in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hyland13"} Hyland (2013)
          %h3#higherorder_abstract_syntax Higher-order abstract syntax
          %p
            Another way of looking at the
            %em free
            cartesian closed category containing a reflexive object is as a representation of pure lambda terms (up to
            = succeed "-equality)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+abstract+syntax"} higher-order abstract syntax
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app : U \to U^U
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"} curried
            form of application, while the operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lam
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lam : U^U \to U
            represents lambda abstraction. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+elements"} global elements
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            can be interpreted as closed lambda terms, and more generally, morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^n \to U
            can be interpreted as terms with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            free variables.
          %h2#references References
          %p Dana Scott’s original work on lattice models of the lambda calculus can be found in:
          %ul
            %li#Scott70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+S.+Scott"}> Dana S. Scott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Outline of a mathematical theory of computation
                %em Fourth Annual Princeton Conference on Information Sciences and Systems
                (1970) 169–176. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://ropas.snu.ac.kr/~kwang/520/readings/sco70.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Scott-TheoryOfComputation.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Scott72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"}> Dana Scott
                \. Continuous Lattices.
                %em Toposes, Algebraic Geometry and Logic
                (ed.: E. Lawvere), pages 97-136, Lecture Notes in Mathematics, vol 274. 1972. doi:
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0073967"} 10.1007/BFb0073967
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/files/3229/PRG07.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Scott76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"}> Dana Scott
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Data types as lattices
                %strong 5
                3 (1976) 522–587 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1137/0205037"}> doi:10.1137/0205037
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/files/3287/PRG05.pdf"} pdf
          %p Scott made the underlying categorical structure explicit in a later paper, where he introduced the definition of a reflexive object in a ccc, and proved a representation theorem for theories of pure lambda calculus:
          %ul
            %li#Scott80
              Dana Scott. Relating theories of the
              = succeed "-calculus." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              %em To H.B. Curry: Essays on Combinatory Logic, Lambda-Calculus and Formalism
              (eds. Hindley and Seldin), Academic Press, 403–450, 1980.
          %p For a modern analysis of Scott’s representation theorem, see:
          %ul
            %li#Hyland13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
                \.
                %em Classical lambda calculus in modern dress
                Mathematical Structures in Computer Science, 27(5) (2017) 762-781. doi:
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129515000377"}> 10.1017/S0960129515000377
                \.
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.5762"} arxiv:1211.5762
            %li#Hyland14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
                ,
                %em Towards a Notion of Lambda Monoid
                , Electronic Notes in Theoretical Computer Science
                %strong 303
                (2014) pp.59-77, doi:
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2014.02.004"} 10.1016/j.entcs.2014.02.004
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} Bicategorical
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            generalizations of the notion of reflexive object are discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
                \.
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Modelling Computations : a 2-categorical Framework
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/WkAdj/LICS.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
                \.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semantics of lambda-I and of other substructure lambda calculi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Typed Lambda Calculi and Applications
              %p
                LNCS 664, 1993, p. 195-208 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0037107"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0037107
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noam+Zeilberger"}> Noam Zeilberger
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear lambda terms as invariants of rooted trivalent maps
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S095679681600023X"} doi:10.1017/S095679681600023X
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1512.06751"} arXiv:1512.06751
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Hugo Paquet
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hugo+Paquet"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Probabilistic concurrent game semantics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/techreports/UCAM-CL-TR-950.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Axel Kerinec, Giulio Manzonetto, and Federico Olimpieri. 2023. Why Are Proofs Relevant in Proof-Relevant Models? Proc. ACM Program. Lang. 7, POPL, Article 8 (January 2023), 31 pages. (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3571201"}> doi:10.1145/3571201
                )
